<header><p class="st-kicker">kingdom · Central and Southern Africa</p><h1 class="st-title">Luvale Kingdom</h1><p class="st-meta">1650 CE – 1905 CE (approx.)</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A Lunda-derived kingdom of the upper Zambezi whose chiefs grew rich from the 19th-century trade in captives and beeswax before colonial partition.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lwena">Luvale</a> (Lovale) trace their ruling line to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lunda-empire">Lunda</a> emigrants who settled along the upper <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zambezi">Zambezi</a> in present-day <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/portuguese-angola">Angola</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zambia">Zambia</a> in the 17th century. The senior chief, Kakenge, held ritual primacy over other Luvale chiefs.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lwena">Luvale</a> chiefs ruled riverine villages of farmers and fishermen, and initiation and masking ceremonies, such as the makishi, were central to social life. In the 19th century <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ovimbundu">Ovimbundu</a> traders and Portuguese-linked merchants from <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/portuguese-angola">Angola</a> bought slaves, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ivory-trade">ivory</a> and beeswax, and the Luvale became active raiders and middlemen, often in conflict with <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/barotseland">Barotseland</a>.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>In 1905 an arbitration by the King of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> divided the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lwena">Luvale</a> lands between <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/portuguese-angola">Portuguese Angola</a> and British <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/northern-rhodesia">Northern Rhodesia</a>. The Luvale remain a people of both <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zambia">Zambia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/angola">Angola</a>, and the makishi masquerade is listed by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco">UNESCO</a>.</p><p class="st-conf">Few sources survive for this state; details are uncertain.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><div class="st-emblem" role="img" aria-label="kingdom, 1650 CE – 1905 CE (approx.)"><svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M22 72 V40 L36 54 L50 28 L64 54 L78 40 V72 Z"/><path d="M22 80 H78"/><circle cx="50" cy="24" r="3"/><circle cx="22" cy="36" r="2.5"/><circle cx="78" cy="36" r="2.5"/></svg><p class="st-emblem-type">kingdom</p><p class="st-emblem-region">Central and Southern Africa</p><div class="st-span" aria-hidden="true"><span class="st-span-bar" style="left:92.54%;width:5.07%"></span></div><div class="st-span-axis" aria-hidden="true"><span>3000 BCE</span><span>1 CE</span><span>2000</span></div></div><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1650 CE – 1905 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>kingdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>tribal</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Traditional Luvale religion</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Luvale</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/angola">Angola</a>, <a href="/countries/zambia">Zambia</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Lovale, Balovale, Kakenge</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1778&amp;focus=luvale-kingdom" aria-label="Luvale Kingdom on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5189 2637 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Luvale Kingdom lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5662 2807h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5509 2893h0"/><path class="map-dot-ap" d="M5509 2893h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>Location approximate: shown in present-day Angola. <a href="/atlas?year=1778&amp;focus=luvale-kingdom">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
